# Review Command

When the user message is **"review"** (or **"reivew"**), or clearly asks to review current session/work/changes, treat it as this instruction:

> Review current session changes to fix possible bugs, remove duplicate or unused code.

## Workspace scope (all projects)

This workspace has **three separate git repos**. Always review **all of them**, not just the repo with the focused file:

1. `ecommerce_admin`
2. `ecommerce_backend`
3. `ecommerce_frontend`

Run `git status` and `git diff` in **each** repo above. Include every modified/untracked file from the session across admin, backend, and frontend.

## Session scope

Review **only changes made in this conversation session** (uncommitted edits). Do not audit unrelated existing code.

## Goals

1. **Fix possible bugs** — logic errors, edge cases, missing null checks, race conditions, type mismatches
2. **Remove duplicate code** — consolidate repeated logic; extract shared helpers only when clearly warranted
3. **Remove unused code** — dead imports, unreachable branches, unused variables/functions

## Process

1. Inspect git diff / session changes in **all three projects**
2. Report findings grouped by project, then by severity (bugs first, then cleanup)
3. Apply fixes by default unless the user asked for review-only
4. Keep changes minimal — no unrelated refactors

## Do not

- Limit the review to a single repo (e.g. backend only)
- Expand scope beyond session changes
- Over-engineer or introduce unnecessary abstractions
